WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.110
♪ You're the sunflower ♪

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:04.695
Narrator: This was the biggest song

00:00:04.695 --> 00:00:06.910
on 2019's biggest album.

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:09.660
"Sunflower" is technically
a Post Malone song,

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.778
but it gets some of its hookiest parts

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:13.870
from featured artist Swae Lee.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.308
Specifically his trademark falsetto.

00:00:16.308 --> 00:00:19.470
♪ Some things you just can't refuse ♪

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.940
"Sunflower" had a historically long stay

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.860
on the top 10 of the hip-hop/R&amp;B charts,

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.420
outlasting the record set by

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:29.470
The Weeknd's 2015 single "The Hills."

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.700
If you look at these two songs,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.720
you start to notice a trend.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.080
Men singing super,

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.410
super high.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:39.630
Here's the thing, though.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.400
This isn't a trend at all.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.430
Falsetto's been sought after in music

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:45.150
since the Byzantine Empire,

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.550
when the church would castrate choirboys

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.471
to preserve the high
pitch of their voices.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:56.588
While the castration practice

00:00:56.588 --> 00:00:58.919
luckily ended in the 1800s,

00:00:58.919 --> 00:01:01.870
falsetto is still a
mainstay in music today.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.380
Turns out, there's a good reason

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.060
we love those high notes.

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.690
It's in our DNA.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.057
We're hardwired to have a strong response

00:01:09.057 --> 00:01:10.892
to falsetto in music

00:01:10.892 --> 00:01:13.130
because of the way our
brains process pitch

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.090
and because of the unique relationship

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:17.495
between falsetto and emotion.

00:01:19.845 --> 00:01:21.553
Justin Stoney: Falsetto is very equivalent

00:01:21.553 --> 00:01:23.110
to the female headvoice.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:26.452
It's all about controlling
teeny, tiny muscles

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:27.960
that are all inside your larynx.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.641
When we're in chest voice,

00:01:30.641 --> 00:01:32.248
vocal chords are making a thick

00:01:32.248 --> 00:01:34.160
and muscular kind of contact.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:35.849
That's a [sings low-pitched note]

00:01:35.849 --> 00:01:37.740
kind of vibration.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.470
Narrator: When you go
into falsetto, though,

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:40.920
the vocal chords stretch out

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.480
and move apart from each other

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.060
so that only their edges are vibrating.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.701
Justin: So, we move from this kind of

00:01:46.701 --> 00:01:47.882
[sings low-pitched note] vibration

00:01:47.882 --> 00:01:49.515
to a [sings high-pitched note]

00:01:49.515 --> 00:01:53.500
lighter, thinner aspect
of their vibration.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.070
Narrator: In this stretched-out position,

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:56.403
your vocal chords are like

00:01:56.403 --> 00:01:58.472
the thin strings on a guitar.

00:01:58.472 --> 00:01:59.810
They can vibrate faster

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.300
and therefore produce higher notes.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.230
So when you shift from your chest voice

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.530
into falsetto,

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:07.990
you can have this huge leap in pitch.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:09.910
That kind of dramatic pitch change

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:12.490
has a big effect on us listeners.

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:15.273
As humans, we primarily
identify each pitch,

00:02:15.273 --> 00:02:17.298
or musical note, that we hear

00:02:17.298 --> 00:02:19.500
through its relation to other pitches,

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.250
like the ones that came before it.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:23.991
This ability is called relative pitch.

00:02:23.991 --> 00:02:26.350
Since relative pitch is intrinsic

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:28.430
to how we perceive not just music

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.120
but dialogue and complex surroundings,

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.840
it makes sense that
large leaps in frequency

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.990
really command our attention.

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:37.700
Bach, the famous composer,

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.175
used this to his advantage.

00:02:39.175 --> 00:02:41.499
He included rapid pitch fluctuations

00:02:41.499 --> 00:02:43.650
in key moments of his compositions

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.228
to make certain flute notes

00:02:45.228 --> 00:02:47.290
pop out to the audience.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:49.120
Pop singers do the same thing,

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.001
but with their vocal melodies.

00:02:51.001 --> 00:02:52.490
Notice that you rarely hear

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:54.860
an entire pop song done in falsetto.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:57.532
Artists usually reserve
their highest registers

00:02:57.532 --> 00:02:59.442
for the big moments of the track.

00:02:59.442 --> 00:03:00.387
Like the hook:

00:03:00.387 --> 00:03:02.577
♪ You just want attention ♪

00:03:02.577 --> 00:03:04.897
♪ You don't want my heart ♪

00:03:04.897 --> 00:03:07.009
Narrator: Or an
emotionally climactic line:

00:03:07.009 --> 00:03:11.575
♪ Cause I give you all ♪

00:03:11.575 --> 00:03:12.723
♪ of me ♪

00:03:12.723 --> 00:03:15.490
Narrator: In this, and
so many other hits...

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:19.279
♪ 'Cause I been thinking 'bout forever ♪

00:03:19.279 --> 00:03:20.112
♪ Ooh ♪

00:03:20.112 --> 00:03:21.810
Narrator: Falsetto is
used to shine a spotlight

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.870
on a particular moment or verse,

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.520
basically telling the
listener, "This is important."

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:28.205
This works thanks in part to

00:03:28.205 --> 00:03:30.540
a little adrenaline rush in the listener.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.865
According to the music cognitive
psychologist David Huron,

00:03:33.865 --> 00:03:36.568
when you hear high-pitched,
loud singing...

00:03:36.568 --> 00:03:38.520
♪ Ahh ♪

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.011
Narrator: Your brain
releases excitatory hormones

00:03:41.011 --> 00:03:42.780
that increase your arousal state

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.750
and make you more attentive.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:47.050
So falsetto gets your attention.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:49.400
But that's not the only
way it affects you.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.530
It also ignites your emotion.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.093
Take one of our most emotional times,

00:03:54.093 --> 00:03:56.780
in the middle of a good ugly cry.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:58.847
When you're sobbing, your voice tends to

00:03:58.847 --> 00:04:01.919
shift up into a kind of unstable falsetto.

00:04:01.919 --> 00:04:05.618
This association carries over into music.

00:04:05.618 --> 00:04:07.878
It's no wonder, then, that falsetto speech

00:04:07.878 --> 00:04:09.365
tends to be identified with

00:04:09.365 --> 00:04:11.630
intense, raw, gut emotions.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:13.070
Depending on the delivery,

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:15.578
falsetto can sound shaky or vulnerable:

00:04:15.578 --> 00:04:18.820
♪ Don't leave me high ♪

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.127
Narrator: Paranoid:

00:04:20.127 --> 00:04:22.391
♪ Before we see it ♪

00:04:22.391 --> 00:04:23.840
♪ You take it ♪

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:25.205
Narrator: Or desperate and pleading:

00:04:25.205 --> 00:04:27.288
♪ The nights are long ♪

00:04:27.288 --> 00:04:28.613
♪ and lonely and ♪

00:04:28.613 --> 00:04:30.090
Narrator: We're biologically primed

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:32.215
to identify it with real,

00:04:32.215 --> 00:04:34.020
raw, gut emotion.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.800
♪ Staying alive ♪

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.660
Narrator: At this point,
you might be wondering:

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:42.118
Why are the most well-known
falsettists all men?

00:04:42.118 --> 00:04:44.080
After all, women can just as easily

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.520
sing in this register.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:47.930
Justin: A big reason is because

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.638
we're used to hearing men in particular

00:04:50.638 --> 00:04:52.410
speak in chest voice.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:54.151
So if you hear a man sort of

00:04:54.151 --> 00:04:55.851
speaking to you in this voice and then

00:04:55.851 --> 00:04:57.210
[switches to high pitch]
suddenly speaking in this voice,

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:59.940
it seems like quite a big contrast,

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.510
and contrast is so huge in pop music.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:04.750
Narrator: So it's all about getting

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:07.090
that striking vocal contrast.

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:08.460
The more dramatic it is,

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.020
the more we pay attention,

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.380
and the more we might respond emotionally.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:15.114
Makes sense that today's
most popular music genre

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:16.690
is reinventing falsetto

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:19.270
by really pushing that contrast.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:21.504
That genre, of course, is hip-hop.

00:05:21.504 --> 00:05:24.770
♪ The truth ♪

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:28.003
♪ Cold like Minnesota,
cold like Minnesota ♪

00:05:28.003 --> 00:05:29.946
Justin: When you've got
lyrics in rap going on

00:05:29.946 --> 00:05:31.504
that are feeling really

00:05:31.504 --> 00:05:33.029
tough and really grounded,

00:05:33.029 --> 00:05:35.143
and then you have a chorus that's

00:05:35.143 --> 00:05:37.343
really airy and light and sweet,

00:05:37.343 --> 00:05:39.690
it's, again, that wonderful contrast.

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:41.540
Narrator: The prevalence of airy falsettos

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:43.982
in songs alongside hard-edge raps...

00:05:43.982 --> 00:05:47.030
♪ Don't wait to jump in too long ♪

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:48.525
Narrator: Shows just how versatile

00:05:48.525 --> 00:05:50.200
this vocal style really is.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:51.470
It's impossible to predict

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:53.470
how falsetto might be reinvented

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:54.810
in the next decade of pop.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:55.980
But one thing is clear:

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:58.549
It's probably not going anywhere.

00:05:58.549 --> 00:06:03.243
♪ I'll be gone ♪

00:06:03.243 --> 00:06:04.576
♪ In a day or ♪

00:06:04.576 --> 00:06:08.644
♪ Two ♪

